{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey rent control framework: no state rent limits, municipal ordinances permitted","requirement":"New Jersey sets no statewide rent control or rent cap; a landlord's rent increases are governed by a municipal rent control or rent leveling ordinance where the town has adopted one, enforced by a locally created board.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential rental units in New Jersey, including in municipalities that adopt rent control or rent leveling ordinances; the state itself does not establish or control rents.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Municipalities that adopt rent control or rent leveling ordinances"},"exemptions":"HUD-owned or HUD-subsidized developments and NJHMFA-financed projects are not subject to municipal rent control; rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J. Dep't of Community Affairs, Truth in Renting, Rent Control/Rent Increases","quoted_span":"The State of New Jersey has no laws that establish, govern or control rents. Municipalities may pass an ordinance establishing rent control or rent leveling.","interaction":"Municipal rent control ordinances govern where adopted; the state does not set or cap rents.","confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law 30-year exemption from local rent control","requirement":"A newly constructed multiple dwelling must be exempt from any local rent control ordinance for 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","key_value":"30 years after completion of construction","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings; exempt from local rent control for 30 years after completion of construction.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"newly constructed multiple dwellings"},"exemptions":"Rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control ordinances.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building","interaction":"Exempts covered buildings from local rent control ordinances.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act limits on rent increases and required notice","requirement":"A landlord may not raise rent during a lease term; the landlord must terminate the old lease, serve written notice and offer a new lease at the increased rent. The increase must not be unconscionable and must comply with municipal rent ordinances.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants under written or oral leases.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1(f)","quoted_span":"The increase in rent must not be unconscionable; it must not be so unreasonable as to shock the conscience of a fair and honest person and must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","interaction":"Rent increases must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Condominium and cooperative conversion rent increase prohibition","requirement":"If a building is converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership of units, a landlord may not increase rents to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in a building converted to condominium or cooperative form of ownership or to fee simple ownership of units.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"converted buildings"},"exemptions":"Rents may still be increased to cover the cost of new services or amenities.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cos ts resulting solely from the conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey Anti-Eviction Act good cause requirement","requirement":"A landlord may evict or refuse to renew a residential lease, written or oral, only for good cause proved in court, and may recover possession only with tenant consent or through the legal eviction process after a Notice to Quit.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential dwelling units; no residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a written or oral lease without good cause.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause. The landlord must be able to prove in court that he has grounds for an eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Security deposit duties of a new owner after sale or transfer","requirement":"When rental property is sold or transferred, the new owner must obtain the tenant's security deposit plus accrued interest from the former owner and is responsible for investing it, giving all notices, paying interest and returning the deposit.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential rental property that is sold or transferred; duties fall on the new owner whether or not the deposit and interest are transferred.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"property sold or transferred"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21","quoted_span":"If the property is sold or transferred it is the duty of the new owner to obtain the security deposit, plus accrued interest on the tenant’s deposit, that was collected by the former owner.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalty for willfully withholding deposits of tenants receiving public assistance","requirement":"A landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds a security deposit made by or on behalf of a tenant receiving State or federal financial assistance, including welfare or rental assistance, may be liable for a civil penalty of $500 to $2,000 per offense.","key_value":"$500 to $2,000 per offense","coverage_conditions":"Tenants who have received financial assistance through any State or federal program, including welfare or rental assistance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"tenants receiving State or federal financial assistance"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense, collected and enforced by summary proceedings under the Penalty Enforcement Law; the State entity that made the deposits receives the penalty.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Criminal penalty for unlawful use of security deposit monies","requirement":"A person who unlawfully uses security deposit monies may be charged as a disorderly person and fined at least $200, imprisoned for up to 30 days, or both.","key_value":"fine not less than $200; up to 30 days imprisonment","coverage_conditions":"Any person who unlawfully uses security deposit monies.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Disorderly person charge; fine of not less than $200 or imprisonment for not more than 30 days, or both.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-25","quoted_span":"a fine of not less than $200 or imprisonment for not more than 30 days, or both (N.J.S.A. 46:8-25).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Landlord may charge credit report cost and reasonable application fees","requirement":"A landlord may charge a prospective tenant the cost of a credit report and may request reasonable rental application fees or employment verification; the text states no cap on these charges.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Prospective tenants screened through credit reports, tenant screening agencies, rental applications or employment verification.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J. Dep't of Community Affairs, Truth in Renting, Credit Checks and Background Checks","quoted_span":"The landlord is allowed to charge the tenant for the cost of the report. The landlord may also request reasonable rental application fees or employment verification.","interaction":"States practice under the federal Fair Credit Reporting Act (15 U.S.C.A. § 1681m).","confidence":0.6,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"LAD protection against discrimination based on source of lawful income","requirement":"A landlord or rental agency may not refuse to rent or discriminate in renting housing because of a renter's source of lawful income, or the other characteristics protected by the Law Against Discrimination.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships in New Jersey, except two-family owner-occupied dwellings and rooms in owner- or resident-occupied single homes.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"single-sex and age-restricted housing as to familial status"},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings; rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home; residences planned for and occupied by one sex, including YMCA and age-restricted housing, as it pertains to familial status.","penalty":"A discriminating landlord may owe monetary damages, may be ordered to rent the unit to the complainant, and faces fines up to $10,000 for a first offense.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
